{
  "date": "2026-10-27",
  "status": "complete",
  "audience": "Curious beginners with no architectural training",
  "scope": 36,
  "recurring_findings": [
    "Concrete openings and everyday scenarios are a strength; preserve them.",
    "Qualifications are repeated across examples, captions, design considerations and limits. Keep each necessary qualification close to the decision it affects.",
    "Unexplained vocabulary includes massing, envelope, conditioned space, bay, section, commissioning and brief. Define terms using the thing a reader can see or do.",
    "Abstract nouns and editorial framing slow the explanation: occupation, legibility, affordances, real comparison and transferable observation.",
    "Photo captions often defend against unsupported claims before explaining the visible mechanism. Lead with what to notice, preserving necessary distinctions and all credits.",
    "Some photo additions disrupted ordinal references such as second example; one image-only comparison heading needs a connecting paragraph.",
    "Long sentences often combine an instruction, several conditions and a caution. Give the main relationship its own sentence.",
    "Repeated boilerplate occupies limited word count. Replace it with useful definitions, clearer contrasts between options and practical consequences."
  ],
  "editorial_rules": [
    "Preserve all ten H2 sections, identities, links, source blocks, recommendation queries and all approved image files.",
    "Maintain 800–1500 substantive words without padding.",
    "Keep cultural, accessibility, technical and evidence limits where they alter interpretation or action.",
    "Keep established names and provenance. No new performance claims or guidance thresholds.",
    "Keep one or two photographic examples per article and no guided practices.",
    "Use counts only to find passages for judgment; do not optimize to a readability score."
  ],
  "pilot": ["arch-site-repair", "arch-drained-ventilated-rainscreen", "arch-test-at-full-scale"],
